batch 1 · checked example f07 — expectation and population variance new teaching capsule and stated examples only; inherited prose/formulas/code remain pending ← previous audit card · next audit card → why this matters an objective averaged over possible outcomes is a weighted sum. it differs from an unweighted sample mean unless the weights justify that mean. prerequisites: stable softmax and log-softmax symbols, shapes and assumptions the values xᵢ are weighted by a normalized pmf pᵢ. population variance uses those probabilities, not an unbiased sample denominator n-1. derivation or mechanism compute μ=σpᵢxᵢ. subtract μ from each possible value. square each difference and take the same weighted average. equivalently use e[x²]-μ². \mu=\mathbb e[x]=\sum_i p_i x_i \mathrm{var}(x)=\sum_i p_i(x_i-\mu)^2=\mathbb e[x^2]-\mu^2 worked numerical for values [0,1,3] with probabilities [0.2,0.5,0.3], μ=0+0.5+0.9=1.4. e[x²]=0+0.5+2.7=3.2, so variance=3.2-1.96=1.24. attention applies a weighted average to each value-vector component. edge cases and limits for vector-valued outcomes, the expectation is componentwise; covariance carries cross-component information. correlated samples do not justify independent-sample success formulas. connections t04 treats attention output as a weighted combination. policy-gradient expectations and group statistics need their own sampling assumptions. source support §3.8, expectation, variance and covariance transformers-slides(1).pdf · physical pdf pages 8 · slide syllabus starting relations are grounded in the cited passages. the stated teaching calculations and derivations are checked independently; these citations do not certify every inherited claim in the lesson. executable companion and checking evidence functions: moments . read or download the complete python companion . checks passed: f07-moments. inspect the check methods . core explanation mark read ← previous lesson · next lesson → prerequisites, purpose, and source status before this: step 0.4 — calculus for deep learning this lesson: step 0.5 — expectation source references: build a reasoning model (from scratch) ; understanding_attention_handbook_01 ; master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs_v2 ; master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs ; rl-grpo-slides ; 5. linear regression original katex syntax checked; claim-by-claim source/mathematical review pending. original teaching retained; independent depth scoring pending. step 0.5 — expectation plan node: i0 · chat order #6 weighted average → expected value → conditional expectation → variance → why e[\cdot] appears everywhere in attention and rl we have now established: 	ext{vectors} ightarrow 	ext{matrices} ightarrow 	ext{probabilities} ightarrow 	ext{softmax} ightarrow 	ext{entropy / ce / kl} ightarrow 	ext{gradients / backpropagation} the next mathematical primitive in your master cheatsheet is:  oxed{e[f(x)]} the cheatsheet gives the discrete definition:  oxed{ e[f(x)] = \sum_x p(x)f(x) } and your rl material later uses expectation directly in objectives such as: j(	heta)=e_{	au\sim\pi_	heta}[r(	au)]. so this is the bridge from basic probability to attention and reinforcement learning . (source: master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs; build a reasoning model (from scratch)) 0.5.1 start with something you already know: an average suppose marks are: 60,70,80 the ordinary average is:  rac{60+70+80}{3}=70 each value receives equal weight:  rac13, rac13, rac13 so we can rewrite it as:  rac13(60)+ rac13(70)+ rac13(80) that is already a weighted average . 0.5.2 expected value is a probability-weighted average suppose instead the outcomes have different probabilities. for a random variable x : outcome x probability p(x=x) 10 0.2 20 0.5 40 0.3 then the expected value is: e[x] = 10(0.2)+20(0.5)+40(0.3) =2+10+12  oxed{e[x]=24} so:  oxed{ 	ext{expectation}= 	ext{average weighted by probability} } this is the most important intuition. 0.5.3 expectation does not necessarily mean a value you will actually observe this is an important subtlety. we got: e[x]=24 but 24 is not one of our possible outcomes. the outcomes are: 10,\;20,\;40 expectation is the long-run weighted average , not necessarily a possible single outcome. imagine repeating the random experiment many times. the average observed value tends toward the expected value under the usual conditions. 0.5.4 random variable a random variable x is a variable whose value depends on a random outcome. for our example: x\in\{10,20,40\} with probabilities: p(x=10)=0.2 p(x=20)=0.5 p(x=40)=0.3 then: e[x] = \sum_x p(x=x)x which is the special case of the more general formula:  oxed{ e[f(x)] = \sum_xp(x=x)f(x) } from your mathematical cheatsheet. (source: master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs) 0.5.5 why do we need f(x) ? because often we don't want the expectation of x itself. we may want: e[x^2] or: e[|x|] or: e[\log x] or: e[r] or: e[a(x)] the general form handles all of these:  oxed{ e[f(x)] = \sum_xp(x)f(x) } 0.5.6 numerical example with f(x)=x^2 use: x= \{1,2,3\} with: p=[0.2,0.5,0.3] first: e[x] = 1(0.2)+2(0.5)+3(0.3) =2.1 now: e[x^2] = 1^2(0.2)+2^2(0.5)+3^2(0.3) =0.2+2+2.7  oxed{e[x^2]=4.9} notice: e[x^2]
eq(e[x])^2 here: (e[x])^2=2.1^2=4.41 while: e[x^2]=4.9 this distinction will become important when we study variance . 0.5.7 expectation and probability distributions suppose: p=[0.1,0.2,0.7] and values: x=[2,5,10] then: e[x] = 0.1(2)+0.2(5)+0.7(10) =0.2+1+7  oxed{e[x]=8.2} so the probability distribution acts as the weighting mechanism. this is a recurring pattern throughout machine learning:  oxed{ 	ext{distribution} ightarrow 	ext{weights} ightarrow 	ext{weighted average} } 0.5.8 this should immediately remind you of attention remember: a=\operatorname{softmax}(s) each row of a contains weights: a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n such that: a_i\ge0 and: \sum_i a_i=1 then attention computes:  oxed{ o=\sum_i a_iv_i } that is mathematically a weighted average of the value vectors. for one query: o= a_1v_1+a_2v_2+\cdots+a_nv_n this is extremely closely related to expectation: e[v] = \sum_ip(i)v_i so a powerful mental model is:  oxed{ 	ext{attention output} \approx 	ext{expectation of values under the attention distribution} } the supplied attention handbook describes attention as a weighted combination of values, with softmax turning scores into normalized weights and av producing the output. (source: understanding_attention_handbook_01) 0.5.9 concrete attention example suppose there are three value vectors: v_1=  egin{bmatrix} 1\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \quad v_2=  egin{bmatrix} 0\ 2 \end{bmatrix}, \quad v_3=  egin{bmatrix} 3\ 1 \end{bmatrix} and attention weights: a=[0.2,0.5,0.3] then: o = 0.2v_1+0.5v_2+0.3v_3 compute the first component: 0.2(1)+0.5(0)+0.3(3) = 1.1 second component: 0.2(0)+0.5(2)+0.3(1) = 1.3 therefore:  oxed{ o=  egin{bmatrix} 1.1\ 1.3 \end{bmatrix} } the attention mechanism has effectively computed: "take the expected value representation under the distribution of attention weights." that's an excellent mathematical interpretation. 0.5.10 but attention weights are not literally an event probability be precise here. we can mathematically treat: [a_1,\ldots,a_n] as a normalized distribution over positions. that allows us to use expectation-like language. but that does not mean: "there is a real-world random event where token 2 occurs with probability 0.5." these are learned coefficients used for aggregation. the attention handbook explicitly distinguishes normalized attention weights from stronger claims about semantic interpretation or explanation. (source: understanding_attention_handbook_01) 0.5.11 expectation of a constant if: x=c always, then: e[x]=c example: x=5 with probability 1. then: e[x]=1(5)=5 so:  oxed{e[c]=c} 0.5.12 linearity of expectation one of the most useful properties is:  oxed{ e[ax+by] = ae[x]+be[y] } for constants a,b . and more generally:  oxed{ e[x+y]=e[x]+e[y] } this is true even when x and y are not independent. that is a very useful fact in probability and reinforcement learning. 0.5.13 numerical example suppose: e[x]=4 e[y]=7 find: e[3x+2y+5] by linearity: e[3x+2y+5] = 3e[x]+2e[y]+5 =3(4)+2(7)+5 =12+14+5  oxed{31} no need to enumerate every possible combination of x and y . 0.5.14 why linearity is so useful in machine learning suppose we have many token losses: l_1,l_2,\ldots,l_n and define: l= rac1n\sum_tl_t we can reason about their expected value using linearity. similarly, in rl we may have total reward: r=r_1+r_2+\cdots+r_t and expectation can be decomposed: e[r] = e[r_1]+\cdots+e[r_t] this becomes very useful when reasoning about returns and policy objectives. 0.5.15 expected reward now enter reinforcement learning. suppose an agent can produce three possible trajectories: trajectory probability reward 	au_1 0.2 10 	au_2 0.5 4 	au_3 0.3 -2 expected reward: e[r] = 0.2(10)+0.5(4)+0.3(-2) =2+2-0.6  oxed{e[r]=3.4} the agent's objective is not: "always get the reward of the single best trajectory." it is often formulated as: maximize expected reward under the policy. your supplied reasoning-model material introduces the policy objective in exactly this expectation form, and the master cheatsheet records:  oxed{ j(	heta)=e_{	au\sim\pi_	heta}[r(	au)] } . (source: build a reasoning model (from scratch); master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs_v2) 0.5.16 why the subscript 	au\sim\pi_	heta ? this notation: e_{	au\sim\pi_	heta}[r(	au)] means: take trajectories 	au generated according to policy \pi_	heta , and average their rewards. so: 	au = trajectory / generated response \pi_	heta = policy parameterized by 	heta r(	au) = reward of trajectory e = average weighted according to the policy's distribution over trajectories this is why expectation is fundamental to rl. 0.5.17 llm as a probability policy for an llm: \pi_	heta(y_t\mid x,y_{<t}) means: probability of choosing token y_t , given the prompt and previous generated tokens. so the model's token probability distribution becomes an rl policy . the supplied master cheatsheet makes this explicit: s_t=(x,y_{<t}) a_t=y_t \pi_	heta(y_t\mid x,y_{<t}) . (source: master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs_v2) therefore: llm probability distribution ↓ policy πθ ↓ generate actions/tokens ↓ trajectory τ ↓ reward ↓ expected reward e[r] 0.5.18 conditional expectation now we need another concept. sometimes the expected value changes depending on known information. for example: what is the expected reward given the current state ? this is:  oxed{ e[x\mid y=y] } called conditional expectation . in a discrete case:  oxed{ e[x\mid y=y] = \sum_x xp(x=x\mid y=y) } the important idea is: first restrict the distribution using the known condition, then calculate the weighted average. 0.5.19 simple example suppose today's weather y is known to be rainy. possible temperature x : temperature p(x\mid rain) 15 0.2 20 0.5 25 0.3 then: e[x\mid rain] = 15(0.2)+20(0.5)+25(0.3) =3+10+7.5  oxed{e[x\mid rain]=20.5} we did not use the unconditional temperature distribution. we used: p(x\mid rain) 0.5.20 why conditional expectation matters for llms an llm constantly computes conditional distributions. for next-token prediction: p(y_t\mid x,y_{<t}) the model is essentially saying: given this context, what is the distribution over possible next tokens? then we can conceptually calculate expected quantities under that distribution. this same conditioning structure appears in: \pi_	heta(y_t\mid x,y_{<t}) during rl. so expectation and conditional probability are naturally connected. 0.5.21 from token-level expectation to sequence-level expectation suppose the model generates a sequence: y_1,y_2,\ldots,y_t the probability of that sequence is: p_	heta(y_{1:t}\mid x) = \prod_{t=1}^{t} p_	heta(y_t\mid x,y_{<t}) the model samples sequences according to this distribution. then rl can evaluate: e_{y\sim\pi_	heta}[r(y)] so:  oxed{ 	ext{token probabilities} ightarrow 	ext{sequence distribution} ightarrow 	ext{expected reward} } that is the mathematical bridge into rl. 0.5.22 variance expectation tells us the center of a distribution. but it doesn't tell us how spread out it is. for that we use variance.  oxed{ \operatorname{var}(x) = e[(x-e[x])^2] } your supplied lecture material uses this exact expectation-based definition when discussing bias-variance. (source: 5. linear regression) 0.5.23 why square the difference? suppose: e[x]=10 and outcomes are: 8,\;10,\;12 the deviations are: -2,\;0,\;+2 if we simply averaged deviations:  rac{-2+0+2}{3}=0 we would incorrectly conclude there is no spread. so we square: (-2)^2=4 0^2=0 2^2=4 which gives a positive measure of spread. 0.5.24 standard deviation variance is in squared units. so we often take the square root:  oxed{ \sigma_x=\sqrt{\operatorname{var}(x)} } this returns the spread to the same units as x . 0.5.25 why does variance matter for our later rl lessons? because rl training estimates expectations from sampled trajectories. sampling introduces noise. we therefore care about:  oxed{	ext{variance of the gradient estimate}} this motivates concepts such as: baselines advantage functions group normalization ppo stabilization grpo's relative normalization your master cheatsheet explicitly notes that the advantage subtracts a baseline to reduce variance under standard conditions, and grpo normalizes group rewards using their standard deviation. (source: master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs_v2) so today's simple expectation/variance lesson directly prepares us for grpo. 0.5.26 expected return in rl suppose an agent receives rewards: r_1,r_2,r_3,\ldots a discounted return is:  oxed{ g_t= \sum_{k=0}^{t-t-1} \gamma^k r_{t+k+1} } where: 0\le\gamma\le1 and \gamma determines how much future rewards are discounted. your master cheatsheet includes exactly this return definition. (source: master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs_v2) then rl often cares about: e[g_t] or expected cumulative reward. so expectation is now appearing again. 0.5.27 why discounting? suppose: \gamma=0.9 and future rewards are: 10,\;10,\;10 then: g = 10+ 0.9(10)+ 0.9^2(10) = 10+9+8.1  oxed{g=27.1} the later rewards still matter, but less. this gives a way to encode the idea: near-term consequences matter more than very distant consequences. we will derive rl returns much more rigorously later; for now the key point is that expectation will be applied over these returns. 0.5.28 monte carlo estimation of expectation here is another concept you absolutely need. suppose: e[x] is difficult to calculate exactly. we can sample: x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n and estimate:  oxed{ e[x] \approx  rac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i } this is the sample mean . for example, suppose rewards from five sampled responses are: [8,6,10,4,7] then estimated expected reward: \hat e[r] =  rac{8+6+10+4+7}{5} =  rac{35}{5}  oxed{\hat e[r]=7} this is how sampled rollouts become useful in rl. 0.5.29 this is exactly the bridge to policy gradients the rl slides explain the key difficulty: we want to optimize an expectation over sampled outputs, but directly differentiating through a discrete token sample is not available. they then use the score-function identity:  oxed{ 
abla_	heta e_{y\sim p_	heta}[a(y)] = e_{y\sim p_	heta} [ a(y)
abla_	heta\log p_	heta(y) ] } this is the mathematical foundation of policy-gradient methods. (source: rl-grpo-slides) don't worry about deriving this yet. just recognize its structure:  oxed{ 
abla(	ext{expectation}) = 	ext{expectation of something} } we will derive this from first principles in the rl section. 0.5.30 the deep connection to attention attention: o=av can be written row-wise as: o_i=\sum_j a_{ij}v_j because: \sum_j a_{ij}=1 this resembles: e[v\mid 	ext{query }i] under the attention distribution. therefore:  oxed{ 	ext{attention} = 	ext{learned weighted aggregation} \approx 	ext{expectation under learned weights} } this is not claiming that attention is formally a stochastic process during ordinary forward inference; it is an extremely useful mathematical interpretation of the weighted aggregation. 0.5.31 the deep connection to rl rl: j(	heta)=e_{	au\sim\pi_	heta}[r(	au)] means:  oxed{ 	ext{average reward over trajectories sampled from the policy} } so attention and rl both use the same fundamental mathematical structure:  oxed{ \sum_i (	ext{weight}_i)(	ext{quantity}_i) } the difference is what the weights represent . attention weights: a_{ij} represent learned attention coefficients over positions. rl weights: \pi_	heta(	au) represent the probability that the policy generates a trajectory. this is one of the most useful unifying observations in this curriculum. 0.5.32 one equation to remember at the highest level:  oxed{ e[f(x)] = 	ext{probability-weighted average of }f(x) } everything else is an application. 0.5.33 summary table concept formula intuition expected value e[x]=\sum_xp(x)x probability-weighted average expected function e[f(x)]=\sum_xp(x)f(x) average of transformed quantity conditional expectation e[x\mid y]=\sum_xxp(x\mid y) average after conditioning variance e[(x-e[x])^2] spread around the mean std. deviation \sqrt{\operatorname{var}(x)} spread in original units sample expectation  rac1n\sum_i f(x_i) estimate expectation from samples attention o=av weighted aggregation / expectation-like operation rl objective j(	heta)=e_{	au\sim\pi_	heta}[r(	au)] expected reward the expectation formula is explicitly in your master mathematical cheatsheet, while the rl material later builds its objective around an expectation over trajectories. (source: master_mathematical_cheatsheet_all_lecture_pdfs; build a reasoning model (from scratch)) 0.5.34 the mathematical spine is now getting connected we can now draw a much richer picture: probability distribution │ ├──→ softmax │ ├──→ entropy │ ├──→ cross-entropy │ ├──→ kl divergence │ └──→ expectation │ ┌───────┴────────┐ ↓ ↓ attention rl │ │ weighted │ aggregation │ ↓ expected reward │ ↓ policy gradient │ ↓ ppo / grpo this is why we are building the foundation in this order. 0.5.35 very important distinctions do not confuse: e[x] with: p(x) probability tells us: how likely an outcome is. expectation tells us: the probability-weighted average of a quantity. likewise don't confuse: e[x] with: e[x^2] or: e[x\mid y] these answer different questions. 0.5.36 one complete example suppose an llm has three possible responses: y_1,y_2,y_3 with probabilities: \pi(y_1)=0.2 \pi(y_2)=0.5 \pi(y_3)=0.3 suppose rewards are: r(y_1)=10 r(y_2)=4 r(y_3)=-2 then: e[r] = 0.2(10)+0.5(4)+0.3(-2) =3.4 the rl objective says: adjust the model so that responses with high reward become more likely, thereby increasing expected reward. that single example is the conceptual precursor to:  oxed{ 	ext{policy gradient} ightarrow 	ext{ppo} ightarrow 	ext{grpo} } 0.5.37 master connection to everything learned so far we can now express the entire foundation as:  oxed{ 	ext{vectors} ightarrow 	ext{matrix operations} ightarrow 	ext{scores} ightarrow 	ext{softmax} ightarrow 	ext{probability distributions} } then:  oxed{ 	ext{probability distributions} ightarrow 	ext{entropy / ce / kl / expectation} } then:  oxed{ 	ext{loss} ightarrow 	ext{gradient} ightarrow 	ext{chain rule} ightarrow 	ext{backpropagation} ightarrow 	ext{parameter update} } and eventually:  oxed{ 	ext{expectation} ightarrow 	ext{rl objective} ightarrow 	ext{policy gradient} ightarrow 	ext{ppo} ightarrow 	ext{grpo} } step 0 foundation — status we've now covered: 0.1 linear algebra primitives  oxed{ 	ext{scalar, vector, matrix, matrix multiplication, dot product, tensor shapes} } 0.2 probability machinery  oxed{ 	ext{probability, log probability, softmax, logits, temperature} } 0.3 information theory  oxed{ 	ext{entropy, cross-entropy, kl divergence} } 0.4 calculus / optimization  oxed{ 	ext{derivative, partial derivative, gradient, chain rule, backpropagation, gradient descent} } 0.5 expectation  oxed{ 	ext{weighted averages, conditional expectation, variance, sample expectation} } there is one final foundation piece we should explicitly establish before jumping into the transformer forward pass:  oxed{	extbf{0.6 — tensor calculus and matrix derivatives}} this is where we make the jump from scalar calculus to actual transformer equations such as: q=xw_q s=qk^t o=av and understand what gradients such as:  rac{\partial l}{\partial w_q} actually mean for matrices/tensors rather than simple scalars. that will make the transition into step 1 — transformer mathematics much more rigorous. master check — step 0.5 try these: q1. what is the difference between an ordinary average and an expectation? q2. given: x=\{10,20,40\} with probabilities: [0.2,0.5,0.3] calculate e[x] . q3. calculate: e[x^2] for the same distribution. q4. is e[x^2] generally equal to (e[x])^2 ? q5. what is the conceptual meaning of: e[x\mid y=y]? q6. given attention weights: [0.2,0.5,0.3] and values: v_1,v_2,v_3 write the attention output as a weighted sum. q7. why can attention be understood as an expectation-like weighted aggregation? q8. what does: e_{	au\sim\pi_	heta}[r(	au)] mean in rl? q9. why is variance important when we estimate an expectation using samples? q10. most important: explain the common mathematical structure between: o=av and: j(	heta)=e_{	au\sim\pi_	heta}[r(	au)].

batch 1 · checked example f08 — matrix gradients from a scalar loss new teaching capsule and stated examples only; inherited prose/formulas/code remain pending ← previous audit card · next audit card → why this matters every parameter matrix needs a gradient with the same shape. deriving it once prevents transpose mistakes throughout q/k/v and lora. prerequisites: matrix multiplication and shape contracts , transpose versus reshape , chain rule and scalar gradients symbols, shapes and assumptions let y=xw and l=½σᵢⱼyᵢⱼ². x is m×n, w is n×p, and dl/dy=y. this fixture is a squared-error teaching loss, not the language-model ce loss. derivation or mechanism changing w[r,j] affects y[i,j] through x[i,r]. sum y[i,j]x[i,r] over i. collect these entries as xᵀy. changing x[i,r] affects y[i,j] through w[r,j]. sum over j to obtain ywᵀ. 
abla_w l=x^	op y,\qquad 
abla_x l=yw^	op worked numerical with the f01 matrices, y=[[2,3],[5,5]]. thus ∇w=[[17,18],[24,26]] and ∇x=[[-1,7],[0,12.5]]. for example, ∂l/∂w₁₁=1×2+3×5=17. the checker perturbs all eight x/w entries separately and compares their loss changes. edge cases and limits a transpose is dictated by index contraction, not inserted until shapes happen to match. if w is stored in the opposite library orientation, convert the formula accordingly. connections f09 updates w. later attention backpropagation and low-rank updates reuse this contraction rule. source support build a large language model (from scratch)(2).pdf · physical pdf pages 589, 593, 594, 595 · appendix a.2/a.4 starting operations; matrix identity independently derived here starting relations are grounded in the cited passages. the stated teaching calculations and derivations are checked independently; these citations do not certify every inherited claim in the lesson. executable companion and checking evidence functions: quadratic_loss , quadratic_gradients . read or download the complete python companion . checks passed: f08-dw, f08-dx, f08-known-gradient. inspect the check methods . batch 1 · checked example f10 — softmax jacobian and the ce logit gradient new teaching capsule and stated examples only; inherited prose/formulas/code remain pending ← previous audit card · next audit card → why this matters this explains why next-token ce training pushes the correct logit up and competing logits down. prerequisites: stable softmax and log-softmax , entropy, cross-entropy, kl, and support , chain rule and scalar gradients symbols, shapes and assumptions for one-hot target y and τ=1, p=softmax(z). the loss is -log p_target. a mean over multiple valid positions introduces its own denominator. derivation or mechanism differentiate the softmax quotient: ∂pᵢ/∂zⱼ=pᵢ(δᵢⱼ-pⱼ). write l=-z_target+log σ exp(z). differentiate to obtain ∂l/∂zⱼ=pⱼ-yⱼ. j_{ij}=p_i(\delta_{ij}-p_j) 
abla_z l=p-y worked numerical for logits [log 1,log 2,log 3], p=[1/6,1/3,1/2]. with target id 2, ce=log 2≈0.693147181 and gradient=[1/6,1/3,-1/2]. the gradients sum to zero, consistent with invariance to adding a common logit offset. edge cases and limits for temperature-scaled logits, the derivative with respect to the unscaled logits gains a 1/τ factor. passing softmax probabilities into a logits-based ce api applies a different computation. connections t01 masks and averages token losses. kd uses soft targets and must specify whether the teacher is fixed. source support build a large language model (from scratch)(2).pdf · physical pdf pages 324, 325, 326 · §5.1; gradient independently derived input/target and reduction contract; api not executed here starting relations are grounded in the cited passages. the stated teaching calculations and derivations are checked independently; these citations do not certify every inherited claim in the lesson. executable companion and checking evidence functions: ce_logits , ce_gradient , softmax_jacobian . read or download the complete python companion . checks passed: f10-ce, f10-ce-gradient, f10-jacobian. inspect the check methods . core explanation mark read ← previous lesson · next lesson → prerequisites, purpose, and source status before this: step 0.5 — expectation this lesson: step 0.6 — tensor / matrix calculus source references: build a large language model (from scratch) original katex syntax checked; claim-by-claim source/mathematical review pending. original teaching retained; independent depth scoring pending. step 0.6 — tensor / matrix calculus plan node: i0 · chat order #7 from scalar derivatives → vector gradients → matrix gradients → transformer gradients this is the last mathematical foundation step before we start the transformer itself. so far we learned:  rac{dl}{dw} for a scalar parameter. but a transformer does not have scalar parameters. it has matrices and tensors such as: w_q,\;w_k,\;w_v,\;w_o,\;w_1,\