abla_w l=  egin{bmatrix}  rac{\partial l}{\partial w_{11}}&  rac{\partial l}{\partial w_{12}}\  rac{\partial l}{\partial w_{21}}&  rac{\partial l}{\partial w_{22}} \end{bmatrix} } so: a matrix gradient has the same shape as the matrix parameter. this is the mental model we will use throughout transformers. 0.6.2 why this matters for transformers remember: q=xw_q from our earlier lessons. the book implements attention through matrix multiplication, including operations such as qk^t and then softmax normalization. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) suppose the final loss is: l we eventually need:  oxed{  rac{\partial l}{\partial w_q} } because w_q is a learnable parameter. the chain is: w_q ightarrow q ightarrow qk^t ightarrow a ightarrow o ightarrow 	ext{transformer} ightarrow 	ext{logits} ightarrow l so the gradient must travel backward through matrix operations . 0.6.3 start with a scalar example suppose: y=wx and: x=3 then: y=3w therefore:  rac{\partial y}{\partial w}=3 simple. now let's increase the number of parameters. 0.6.4 vector parameter suppose: \mathbf w=  egin{bmatrix} w_1\ w_2 \end{bmatrix} and: l=w_1^2+3w_2^2 then:  rac{\partial l}{\partial w_1}=2w_1 and:  rac{\partial l}{\partial w_2}=6w_2 therefore:  oxed{ 
