ections have received low-rank trainable updates. 3.3.8 a small numerical shape example suppose: n=8, \qquad d=16, \qquad d_k=4. for one attention projection: w_q\in\mathbb{r}^{16	imes4}. take: r=2. then: b_q\in\mathbb{r}^{16	imes2} and: a_q\in\mathbb{r}^{2	imes4}. therefore: b_qa_q has shape: (16	imes2)(2	imes4) =  oxed{16	imes4}. exactly the shape of: w_q. 3.3.9 parameter count for w_q original: 16	imes4=64 parameters. lora: 2(16+4) = 40. so for this deliberately small example, the reduction is:  rac{64}{40}=1.6. notice something important: lora is only strongly parameter-efficient when r is much smaller than the layer dimensions. that is why the condition:  oxed{ r\ll d,d_{	ext{out}} } matters. 3.3.10 now use a realistic hidden size the peft lecture uses: d=4096 and: r=8. for a square attention projection: w_q\in\mathbb{r}^{4096	imes4096}. full parameter count: 4096^2 =  oxed{16,777,216}. lora parameter count: 8(4096+4096) = 8(8192) =  oxed{65,536}. this is the source's exact numerical example. (source: peft-slides) 3.3.11 reduction for one projection  rac{16,777,216}{65,536} =  oxed{256}. so instead of training: 16.78	ext{ million} parameters for one projection, we train: 65,536. the reduction is:  oxed{256	imes}. 3.3.12 but one transformer layer has four projections the source applies lora to:  oxed{ w_q,w_k,w_v,w_o. } so there are: 4 such matrices. each requires: 65,536 lora parameters. therefore: 4	imes65,536 =  oxed{262,144} trainable parameters per layer. 3.3.13 now 32 transformer layers suppose: l=32. then: 32	imes262,144 =  oxed{8,388,608}. therefore:  oxed{ 8,388,608 } lora parameters are trainable across all four attention projections of all 32 layers. this exact full-model calculation appears in your peft lecture. (source: peft-slides) 3.3.14 compare against the full model the source's model is described as:  oxed{6.74b} total parameters. lora trains: 8,388,608. calculate the fraction:  rac{8,388,608}{6.74	imes10^9}. approximately: 0.001244. as a percentage: 0.1244\%. so:  oxed{ \approx0.124\% } of the model parameters are trained. the remaining approximately:  oxed{99.876\%} are frozen under this setup. this is the exact percentage highlighted by the peft lecture. (source: peft-slides) 3.3.15 this is the key full-model numerical memorize the derivation , not just the answer. given: d=4096, \quad r=8, \quad l=32. one projection: r(2d) = 8(8192) = 65,536. four projections: 4(65,536) = 262,144. 32 layers: 32(262,144) =  oxed{8,388,608}. therefore:  oxed{ 8.39	ext{ million trainable parameters}. } 3.3.16 exam question a 32-layer transformer has hidden size 4096 . lora rank is 8 . lora is applied to w_q,w_k,w_v,w_o in every layer. how many trainable lora parameters are introduced? step 1 — one projection 8(4096+4096) = 65,536. step 2 — four projections 4(65,536) = 262,144. step 3 — 32 layers 32(262,144) =  oxed{8,388,608}. 3.3.17 exam question — what if rank doubles? suppose: r=16 instead of: r=8. because: n_{	ext{lora}}\propto r, the number doubles: 8,388,608	imes2 =  oxed{16,777,216}. so:  oxed{ r\uparrow2	imes ightarrow n_{	ext{trainable}}\uparrow2	imes. } 3.3.18 exam question — what if layers double? suppose: l=64 instead of 32, with everything else unchanged. since: n\propto l, we get: 8,388,608	imes2 =  oxed{16,777,216}. therefore:  oxed{ n_{	ext{lora}}\propto lr } when the target dimensions and number of target matrices per layer remain fixed. 3.3.19 general full-model formula suppose: l = number of layers, m = number of targeted matrices per layer, each matrix has dimensions d	imes d_{	ext{out}} , lora rank r . then:  oxed{ n_{	ext{lora,total}} = l\,m\,r(d+d_{	ext{out}}). } for the source example: l=32 m=4 r=8 d=d_{	ext{out}}=4096. therefore: 32	imes4	imes8(4096+4096) =  oxed{8,388,608}. this formula is one of the most useful exam formulas from this section. 3.3.20 important: number of heads does not appear here you might ask: "the transformer has multiple attention heads. why didn't we multiply by the number of heads?" because in the standard combined projection representation: w_q\in\mathbb{r}^{d	imes d} already produces the projections for all heads. for example: d=4096. if: h=32, then each head might have: d_h= rac{4096}{32}=128. but the combined w_q is still: 4096	imes4096. so you do not multiply its parameter count by 32 again. this is an important exam trap. 3.3.21 let's see why the combined projection produces: q=xw_q. suppose: x:n	imes4096 and: w_q:4096	imes4096. then: q:n	imes4096. afterward, reshape: n	imes4096 into: 32	imes n	imes128. so the head separation happens after the projection. therefore:  oxed{ w_q	ext{ already contains all head projections}. } 3.3.22 connection to our earlier mha lesson previously we learned: d_h= rac{d}{h}. for: d=4096,\quad h=32, we get: d_h=128. the q tensor can be viewed as: q\in\mathbb{r}^{n	imes32	imes128} or, depending on convention: 32	imes n	imes128. but the lora target matrix before reshaping is:  oxed{ w_q:4096	imes4096. } this connects the lora lesson directly back to the transformer mathematics we already completed. 3.3.23 what about w_o ? after attention: 	ext{concat(head}_1,\ldots,	ext{head}_h) returns to model width: n	imes d. then: w_o\in\mathbb{r}^{d	imes d} projects it. so for: d=4096, we again have: 4096	imes4096. therefore w_o receives exactly the same lora parameter count in the source's simplified model:  oxed{65,536}. 3.3.24 full one-layer picture for one transformer layer: x │ ┌─────────┼─────────┐ │ │ │ wq wk wv │ │ │ +lora +lora +lora │ │ │ ▼ ▼ ▼ q k v \ │ / \ │ / attention │ ▼ concat heads │ wo +lora │ ▼ attention output so lora is attached to the linear projections, while the attention operation itself remains the same. 3.3.25 does lora change the causal mask? no. the causal mask is still: m_{ij} =  egin{cases} 0,&j\le i\ -\infty,&j>i. \end{cases} lora changes the learned projections: w_q,w_k,w_v,w_o. it does not remove the autoregressive constraint. therefore:  oxed{ 	ext{lora adaptation}
eq	ext{change to causal masking}. } the attention mathematics remains: a= \operatorname{softmax} \left(  rac{qk^t}{\sqrt{d_k}}+m ight). 3.3.26 does lora change the softmax? again:  oxed{	ext{no.}} the ordinary attention calculation remains. lora changes: q,\ k,\ v through their projections. then the usual: qk^t and softmax operate on those adapted representations. 3.3.27 does lora change the transformer architecture? at the conceptual level:  oxed{ 	ext{no major architectural redesign is required.} } you have: 	ext{same transformer} + 	ext{small trainable low-rank branches}. this is why peft is attractive: the pretrained architecture remains largely intact. 3.3.28 lora training flow now imagine training. step 1 load pretrained model: w_0. step 2 freeze: w_0. step 3 insert lora factors: a,b. step 4 initialize them according to the lora scheme. step 5 run forward pass: xw_0+ rac{\alpha}{r}(xb)a. step 6 calculate task loss: l. step 7 backpropagate. gradients update:  oxed{a,b} but not:  oxed{w_0}. 3.3.29 which parameters receive gradients? for full fine-tuning: 
