eq f(n). } 3.3.36 exam trap — do not multiply by batch size similarly: b=	ext{batch size} does not multiply the number of lora parameters. the same lora matrices are shared across every example in the batch. therefore:  oxed{ n_{	ext{lora parameters}} 	ext{ is independent of batch size}. } 3.3.37 the four different dimensions you must keep separate this is particularly important for numerical questions.  oxed{d} = model/hidden dimension.  oxed{d_h} = per-head dimension.  oxed{r} = lora rank.  oxed{n} = sequence length. do not substitute one for another. for example: d=4096, \quad h=32, \quad d_h=128, \quad r=8, \quad n=2048. all five numbers have completely different meanings. 3.3.38 full numerical shape problem suppose: n=128 d=4096 h=32 d_h=128 r=8. for w_q : w_q:4096	imes4096. lora: b_q:4096	imes8 a_q:8	imes4096. input: x:128	imes4096. then: xb_q: (128	imes4096)(4096	imes8) =  oxed{128	imes8}. then: (xb_q)a_q: (128	imes8)(8	imes4096) =  oxed{128	imes4096}. this is added to: xw_q: (128	imes4096)(4096	imes4096) =  oxed{128	imes4096}. everything is dimensionally consistent. 3.3.39 now connect it to the attention heads the resulting: q:128	imes4096 is reshaped into:  oxed{ 32	imes128	imes128 } depending on tensor ordering. that's: h	imes n	imes d_h. so: 32	imes128	imes128 = 524,288 elements, which is exactly: 128	imes4096. again, the lora operation happened before the head split in this standard combined-projection representation. 3.3.40 what the source actually establishes the strongest source-derived result for this step is the concrete peft example:  oxed{ w_q,w_k,w_v,w_o } with: d=4096,\quad r=8, across: 32 layers, giving:  oxed{ 8,388,608 } trainable lora parameters out of:  oxed{ 6.74b } total parameters, or approximately:  oxed{ 0.124\%. } (source: peft-slides) the source does not establish that every transformer architecture should always target exactly these four matrices. so for architecture-specific target-module choices, we should treat them as configuration choices rather than a universal theorem. 3.3.41 master formula for this step for l layers, m target matrices per layer, matrix shape d	imes d_{	ext{out}} , and rank r :  oxed{ n_{	ext{lora}} = lmr(d+d_{	ext{out}}) } for square attention projections: d=d_{	ext{out}}, therefore:  oxed{ n_{	ext{lora}} = 2lmrd. } for the supplied example: 2(32)(4)(8)(4096) =  oxed{8,388,608}. 3.3.42 master mental model at the model level : pretrained transformer │ freeze w0 everywhere │ ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐ │ │ │ wq wk wv │ │ │ + lora + lora + lora │ │ │ └──────────────┼──────────────┘ │ attention │ ▼ wo │ + lora │ ▼ transformer output and mathematically:  oxed{ w_i' = w_i+ rac{\alpha}{r}b_ia_i } for each selected projection i . 3.3.43 cumulative numerical bank you should now be able to solve: q1 d=4096,\quad r=8. one square projection:  oxed{65,536} lora parameters. q2 four projections:  oxed{262,144} per layer. q3 32 layers:  oxed{8,388,608}. q4 percentage of 6.74b:  oxed{\approx0.124\%}. q5 if r doubles:  oxed{	ext{lora parameters double}}. q6 if layers double:  oxed{	ext{lora parameters double}}. q7 if target matrices change from 4 to 2:  oxed{	ext{lora parameters halve}}. q8 does sequence length change trainable parameter count?  oxed{	ext{no}}. q9 does number of heads require another multiplication?  oxed{	ext{no, not for the combined }d	imes d	ext{ projection representation}.} q10 what remains frozen?  oxed{	ext{the pretrained base weights }w_0}. 3.3.44 the three layers of understanding at this point you should see lora at three levels. level 1 — matrix  oxed{ w'=w_0+ rac{\alpha}{r}ba } level 2 — transformer module  oxed{ w_q,w_k,w_v,w_o ightarrow 	ext{lora adaptations} } level 3 — whole model  oxed{ n=lmr(d+d_{	ext{out}}) } this is exactly the progression you need for exam numericals. step 3.3 complete we have now connected the abstract lora factorization to the actual transformer. the central chain is:  oxed{ x ightarrow w_q,w_k,w_v ightarrow q,k,v ightarrow 