core explanation mark read ← previous lesson · next lesson → prerequisites, purpose, and source status before this: step 0.3 — entropy → cross-entropy → kl divergence , step 2.2 — temperature scaling this lesson: step 4.1 — knowledge distillation: why, teacher–student paradigm, hard vs soft distillation source references: build a deepseek model (from scratch) ; build a reasoning model (from scratch) original katex syntax checked; claim-by-claim source/mathematical review pending. original teaching retained; independent depth scoring pending. step 4.1 — knowledge distillation: why, teacher–student paradigm, hard vs soft distillation plan node: i5 · chat order #33 we are now entering the knowledge distillation section. the big question is: how can a small model learn behavior that a much larger model has already learned, without having to rediscover everything from scratch? the supplied build a deepseek model (from scratch) chapter frames distillation as the final compression step after the reasoning model has been trained: a large teacher transfers useful behavior to a smaller student . it distinguishes hard distillation from soft distillation , and later connects this to the deepseek-r1 distilled models. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch); build a reasoning model (from scratch)) 1. first understand the deployment problem suppose we have a huge reasoning model. in the chapter's deepseek example:  oxed{671	ext{ billion parameters}} are in the full deepseek-r1 model. although the moe model activates only a fraction of those parameters for a token, the full weights still need to be stored and accessible. the chapter gives approximately:  oxed{1342	ext{ gb}} to store the 671b parameters in fp16. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) that creates a problem. a model can be:  oxed{	ext{very capable}} but simultaneously:  oxed{	ext{very expensive to deploy}}. we therefore want:  oxed{ 	ext{large powerful teacher} ightarrow 	ext{small capable student} } 2. what is knowledge distillation? the chapter defines knowledge distillation as a model-compression technique in which a smaller student is trained to reproduce the behavior of a larger teacher , transferring its learned knowledge into a more compact model. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) so we have: teacher t large, capable, already trained. student s smaller, cheaper, needs to learn. the goal is not:  oxed{s=t	ext{ architecturally}} the student can have a completely different and much smaller architecture. instead:  oxed{ s(x)\approx t(x) } in the behavior we care about. 3. teacher is frozen this is fundamental. during distillation:  oxed{	heta_t=	ext{fixed}} while:  oxed{	heta_s=	ext{trainable}}. so the teacher is not learning from the student. the direction is:  oxed{ 	ext{teacher}ightarrow	ext{student} } not: 	ext{teacher}\leftrightarrow	ext{student}. the chapter explicitly describes the teacher as frozen. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 4. why not just train the student on the ordinary labels? suppose the correct class is: 	ext{cat}. a standard hard label is:  oxed{ y=[1,0,0,0] } for: [	ext{cat},	ext{dog},	ext{fox},	ext{bird}]. what does the student learn? only: cat is the correct answer. it does not directly receive information about: whether dog is somewhat plausible, whether fox is slightly plausible, whether bird is almost impossible. the teacher can provide that additional information. 5. hard labels vs teacher probabilities imagine the teacher produces:  oxed{ [0.86,\ 0.12,\ 0.015,\ 0.005] } for: [	ext{cat},	ext{dog},	ext{fox},	ext{bird}]. now the student receives much richer information. the teacher is effectively saying: 	ext{cat} \gg 	ext{dog} > 	ext{fox} > 	ext{bird}. the chapter calls the information contained in these relative probabilities dark knowledge . (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 6. why is that useful? suppose two wrong classes are: 	ext{dog}=0.12 and: 	ext{bird}=0.005. these probabilities communicate:  oxed{ 	ext{dog is much closer to cat than bird is} } according to what the teacher has learned. a hard label throws that information away: [1,0,0,0]. the teacher's output preserves the relational structure. 7. think of the teacher as giving a richer answer hard label: “the answer is cat.” soft teacher output: “the answer is cat. dog is somewhat plausible. fox is much less plausible. bird is almost impossible.” so:  oxed{ 	ext{hard label}=	ext{answer} } while:  oxed{ 	ext{soft target}=	ext{answer + relative alternatives} } this is the central intuition behind classical distillation. 8. hard distillation the first approach is hard distillation . the teacher generates an output sequence: y_t=(y_1,y_2,\ldots,y_m). the student is trained to reproduce those tokens. mathematically, this is simply standard language-model cross-entropy:  oxed{ l_{	ext{hard}} = -\sum_t \log p_s(y_t\mid y_{<t},x) } or averaged over tokens:  oxed{ l_{	ext{hard}} = - rac1m \sum_t \log p_s(y_t\mid y_{<t},x) } the student therefore sees:  oxed{	ext{teacher-generated text}} as its supervision. the chapter explicitly describes hard distillation as training on teacher-generated tokens and notes that this is essentially supervised fine-tuning on synthetic data. (source: build a reasoning model (from scratch)) 9. example of hard distillation for an llm prompt: solve 2x+4=10 . teacher generates: 2x + 4 = 10 2x = 6 x = 3 tokenized: [t_1,t_2,t_3,\ldots,t_m]. student training is just: t_1ightarrow t_2 t_1,t_2ightarrow t_3 etc. so the teacher's reasoning trace becomes a supervised training sequence. 10. why hard distillation is attractive you do not need access to:  oxed{	ext{teacher logits}} you only need:  oxed{	ext{teacher-generated text}}. therefore the teacher can be used to create a dataset offline: teacher | | generate ▼ synthetic dataset | ▼ student sft the teacher can then be removed from the student-training process. this becomes extremely important in the deepseek-r1 approach later. 11. soft distillation now consider the more classical approach. instead of using only the teacher's selected token:  oxed{y_t} we use the teacher's complete probability distribution over the vocabulary. suppose the vocabulary has: |v|=50,000. at one position the teacher gives: p_t(w_1),p_t(w_2),\ldots,p_t(w_{50,000}). the student is trained to approximate this entire distribution. so:  oxed{ p_s(\cdot\mid x) \approx p_t(\cdot\mid x) } 12. hard vs soft mathematically hard distillation student learns:  oxed{ p_s(y_{	ext{teacher}}) } for the teacher-selected token. soft distillation student learns:  oxed{ p_s(w)\approx p_t(w) \quad orall w\in v } the second contains much more information. 13. why kl divergence appears we need a mathematical way to compare: p_t and: p_s. the chapter uses:  oxed{	ext{kl divergence}} for this purpose. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) the teacher distribution is the reference: p_t and the student's goal is to make: p_s look like it. the kl divergence is:  oxed{ d_{kl}(p_t\|p_s) = \sum_i p_t(i) \log  rac{p_t(i)}{p_s(i)} } and we minimize it with respect to the student's parameters. 14. why direction matters you may see: d_{kl}(p_t\|p_s) rather than: d_{kl}(p_s\|p_t). in the supplied chapter, the soft-target loss is explicitly described as:  oxed{ kl(	ext{teacher}\parallel	ext{student}) } and implemented using teacher probabilities and student log-probabilities. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) that corresponds to the standard pytorch-style computation: soft_teacher = f.softmax(teacher_logits / t, dim=-1) soft_student = f.log_softmax(student_logits / t, dim=-1) kl_loss = f.kl_div( soft_student, soft_teacher, reduction="batchmean" ) the important exam interpretation is:  oxed{	ext{teacher provides the target distribution}} and:  oxed{	ext{student is adjusted to match it}}. 15. tiny kl numerical example suppose: p_t=[0.7,0.2,0.1] and: p_s=[0.6,0.3,0.1]. then: d_{kl}(p_t\|p_s) = 0.7\log rac{0.7}{0.6} + 0.2\log rac{0.2}{0.3} + 0.1\log rac{0.1}{0.1}. the final term is: 0.1\log1=0. so: d_{kl} \approx 0.7(0.1542)+0.2(-0.4055) \approx0.108-0.081  oxed{\approx0.027}. the smaller the mismatch between teacher and student distributions, the smaller the kl divergence. 16. why not simply use mean squared error on probabilities? you could mathematically compare: \sum_i(p_t(i)-p_s(i))^2. but classical distillation uses kl divergence because we're comparing probability distributions , and kl gives a natural information-theoretic mismatch measure. the supplied chapter specifically grounds soft distillation in kl divergence. (source: build a reasoning model (from scratch)) 17. attempt #1: ignore the teacher the chapter builds its distillation loss progressively. attempt #1 train the student exactly as a normal model:  oxed{ l=l_{	ext{hard}} } with ground-truth labels. this establishes the baseline. the student learns, but must independently discover relationships that the teacher already knows. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 18. attempt #2: use teacher probabilities at t=1 a natural idea is: l=d_{kl}(p_t\|p_s) using ordinary softmax. but there's a problem. the teacher distribution may already be extremely peaked. for the example from the chapter: z=[5,3,1,-1]. at: t=1 the teacher probabilities are approximately:  oxed{ [0.865,\ 0.117,\ 0.016,\ 0.002] } (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) compare this with the hard target: [1,0,0,0]. they're not identical, but they are quite similar in shape. the probabilities: 0.016,\quad0.002 are tiny. so much of the dark knowledge is still hidden. the chapter calls this attempt #2 and explains that the improvement over hard-label training is small. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 19. why are tiny probabilities a problem? suppose the teacher says: p_t(	ext{fox})=0.016. that tells us fox is somewhat possible. but the gradient signal associated with such a tiny value is much weaker than the dominant class signa