ce: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 5.12.16 stage 2 — reasoning rl now the model is: 	ext{dev-1}. apply:  oxed{	ext{reasoning rl}} using rule-based/verifiable rewards. this creates:  oxed{	ext{dev-2}}. the basic reasoning rl is similar to r1-zero, but there is one important addition:  oxed{	ext{language-consistency reward}}. (source: deepseek-r1-slides) 5.12.17 what is language-consistency reward? suppose the target language is english. a response has 50 words in its reasoning. if: 45 are english: r_{	ext{language}} =  rac{45}{50} =  oxed{0.90}. for another response: 33 out of: 60 are english: r_{	ext{language}} =  rac{33}{60} =  oxed{0.55}. these exact examples appear in the supplied r1 slides. (source: deepseek-r1-slides) 5.12.18 general formula the language reward is:  oxed{ r_{	ext{language}} =  rac{ n_{	ext{target-language words}} }{ n_{	ext{total words}} } } so: 0\le r_{	ext{language}}\le1. a value close to: 1 means strong consistency with the target language. 5.12.19 why add this reward? because otherwise the model may generate something like: english reasoning → chinese reasoning → english → chinese → english while still getting the correctness reward. from the model's optimization perspective:  oxed{ 	ext{language consistency is irrelevant unless rewarded} } therefore the reward must encode the behavior we want. 5.12.20 reward composition conceptually:  oxed{ r_{	ext{total}} = r_{	ext{reasoning}} + r_{	ext{language}} } for this stage as depicted in the supplied slides. the important tradeoff is that adding the language-consistency reward causes a slight accuracy regression , which the source describes as a deliberate readability tradeoff. (source: deepseek-r1-slides) this is a beautiful practical lesson:  oxed{ 	ext{better reward alignment} 
