k	ext{ non-reasoning samples}}. total:  oxed{\sim800k}. (source: deepseek-r1-slides) 5.12.26 why not use only reasoning data? because that creates another failure mode. imagine training almost entirely on: 	ext{math + code + logic}. the model may become excellent at these tasks but worse at: ordinary conversation, writing, editing, factual qa, general instruction following. the source explicitly describes this as a reason for adding broader non-reasoning data. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) so the dataset is deliberately mixed:  oxed{ 600k	ext{ reasoning} + 200k	ext{ general} } 5.12.27 stage 3 sft the combined dataset is used to fine-tune: 	ext{deepseek-v3-base} to produce:  oxed{	ext{dev-3}}. the supplied slides state:  oxed{	ext{2 epochs}} over approximately 800k examples. (source: deepseek-r1-slides) conceptually:  oxed{ 	ext{dev-2 outputs} ightarrow 	ext{filter} ightarrow 	ext{600k reasoning} } plus:  oxed{ 	ext{200k general data} } then:  oxed{ 	ext{sft} ightarrow 	ext{dev-3}. } 5.12.28 why this stage is so clever stage 2 says: 	ext{“discover stronger reasoning.”} stage 3 says: 	ext{“turn the discovered behavior into reusable supervised knowledge.”} so:  oxed{ 	ext{rl} ightarrow 	ext{data generation} ightarrow 	ext{sft} } this is the same fundamental bridge we later use for distillation. 5.12.29 stage 4 — final rl now we have a model that is: good at reasoning, better formatted, better at general tasks. but the problem becomes: how do we align the final model with broader human preferences? this requires more than deterministic math/code verifiers. so the final stage uses a:  oxed{	ext{hybrid reward}}. (source: deepseek-r1-slides; build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 5.12.30 reward source depends on the task for math/code/logic:  oxed{ r_{	ext{reasoning}} = 	ext{rule-based verifier} } for open-ended tasks:  oxed{ r_{	ext{general}} = 	ext{learned reward model} } the supplied book specifically explains that a deterministic verifier is exce