d mc4 used in multilingual pretraining. (source: indicxtreme-slides) this is a fundamental issue. you cannot transfer useful representations for a language that the model barely encountered or did not represent in its pretraining resources. 5.26.3 think of model capacity as a budget suppose a model has a finite representational capacity: c. it must distribute that capacity across languages: c=c_1+c_2+\cdots+c_l. for a dominant language such as english: c_{	ext{english}} can be very large. for a very low-resource language: c_{	ext{low-resource}} may be tiny. therefore:  oxed{ 	ext{multilingual} 
eq 	ext{equally represented} } this is an important conceptual point from the deck's “starved languages” discussion. (source: indicxtreme-slides) the equation above is a conceptual model, not a formula presented by the source. 5.26.4 problem 2 — even supported languages are starved suppose a language technically exists in the pretraining corpus. that does not mean it receives enough data. the second problem is:  oxed{ 	ext{language exists} 
