eq 	ext{language capability that has actually been benchmarked} } 5.26.7 the three-problem model this gives us the foundation of indicxtreme:  oxed{ 	ext{poor model coverage} } +  oxed{ 	ext{insufficient data for supported languages} } +  oxed{ 	ext{poor benchmark coverage} } \downarrow  oxed{ 	ext{need a dedicated indic benchmark} } this is the conceptual starting point of the deck. (source: indicxtreme-slides) 5.26.8 what exactly is indicxtreme trying to cover? the deck describes three related contributions from the original work:  oxed{	ext{indiccorp v2}}  oxed{	ext{indicxtreme}}  oxed{	ext{indicbert v2}} (source: indicxtreme-slides) let's understand why these three belong together. indiccorp v2 a monolingual indic pretraining corpus:  oxed{20.9	ext{ billion tokens}} across:  oxed{24	ext{ languages}} and:  oxed{4	ext{ language families}}. its purpose is to provide better indic-language pretraining resources. (source: indicxtreme-slides) indicxtreme the benchmark contains:  oxed{9	ext{ tasks}} across:  oxed{20	ext{ languages}} with human supervision. the deck says there are:  oxed{105	ext{ evaluation sets}} in total, of which:  oxed{52} are brand-new contributions. (source: indicxtreme-slides) indicbert v2 a:  oxed{278	ext{m-parameter}} bert model pretrained on indiccorp v2 and evaluated using indicxtreme. so the logical chain is:  oxed{ 	ext{better indic data} ightarrow 	ext{indic-specialized model} ightarrow 	ext{indic-specific evaluation} } (source: indicxtreme-slides) 5.26.9 why are there 24 languages in indiccorp but 22 scheduled languages? this is a useful place to be careful with source terminology. the deck says the paper targets the:  oxed{22	ext{ languages of the 8th schedule}} but indiccorp v2 itself contains:  oxed{24	ext{ languages}}. later, the deck notes that indicxtreme can evaluate only 19 of indiccorp's 24 languages, leaving five without an evaluation set. (source: indicxtreme-slides) so do not collapse all the numbers into “24 indian languages