atement: 22-15 =  oxed{7}. this matches the slide's statement that no xtreme-r task covers more than seven indic languages. (source: indicxtreme-slides) the important lesson is not merely the number 7. it is:  oxed{ 	ext{a benchmark can systematically hide failures by omitting languages} } 5.26.14 exam numerical: corpus growth indiccorp v1: 8.79	ext{b tokens}. indiccorp v2: 20.9	ext{b tokens}. the ratio is:  rac{20.9}{8.79} \approx  oxed{2.38	imes}. the deck reports exactly this value and notes that the paper rounds it to about 2.3×. (source: indicxtreme-slides) this is an excellent exam-style calculation. 5.26.15 exam numerical: post-filter reduction before offensive-content filtering: 23.1	ext{b tokens}. after filtering: 20.9	ext{b tokens}. reduction: 23.1-20.9 =  oxed{2.2	ext{b tokens}}. percentage:  rac{2.2}{23.1}	imes100 \approx  oxed{9.52\%}. the deck reports this as approximately:  oxed{9.5\%}. (source: indicxtreme-slides) 5.26.16 the key benchmarking philosophy now we can formulate the problem properly. a multilingual benchmark should not merely ask: 	ext{“can the model do nlp?”} it should ask:  oxed{ 	ext{“can the model do nlp across languages with drastically different resource levels?”} } and for indic languages, the benchmark needs:  oxed{ 	ext{broad language coverage} + 	ext{human-supervised data} + 	ext{multiple task types} } the deck's next section therefore moves into the 9-task indicxtreme suite . (source: indicxtreme-slides) 5.26.17 one subtle distinction to remember there are really three different problems :  oxed{	ext{data problem}} does the model have enough training text?  oxed{	ext{representation problem}} does the model/tokenizer adequately represent the language?  oxed{	ext{evaluation problem}} do we actually have a benchmark that measures the capability? indicxtreme primarily attacks the third problem, while the overall paper also contributes indiccorp v2 to address the first and indicbert v2 to demonstrate the value of indic-focused pretr