on can hurt transfer imagine a model has learned representations shared across: l_1,l_2,l_3,\ldots,l_{29}. now training overwhelmingly on l_1 changes the optimization pressure. the model is pushed toward:  oxed{ 	ext{perform extremely well on }l_1 } rather than:  oxed{ 	ext{maintain broad multilingual generalization} }. this can lead to specialization without broad transfer. the source gives empirical evidence of exactly this pattern with airavata. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.34.28 an important connection to catastrophic forgetting we should be careful here. the supplied deck does not explicitly analyze airavata in terms of catastrophic forgetting , so we should not claim that this experiment proves catastrophic forgetting. a reasonable interpretation is that highly concentrated fine-tuning can reduce transfer to other languages, but the source's supported conclusion is simply:  oxed{ 	ext{single-language specialization does not transfer effectively to the rest} }. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.34.29 resource-tier connection now connect this to everything from steps 5.29 and 5.33. we already found:  oxed{ 	ext{high resource} > 	ext{medium} > 	ext{low} } in generation performance. (source: indicgenbench-slides) and now:  oxed{ 	ext{hindi specialization} 
