eq 	ext{factual error} } and qualitative analysis is needed to distinguish them. 5.35.9 exam question question: why can a generation metric alone fail to fully diagnose hallucination? because a numerical similarity score tells us how similar the generated output is to the reference under that metric, but qualitative inspection is needed to determine whether the model introduced unsupported facts that were not in the source. the indicgenbench authors explicitly use manual analysis to expose such errors. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.10 failure mode 2 — language bleeding now comes a very interesting multilingual failure:  oxed{	ext{language bleeding}} the deck focuses on:  oxed{	ext{awadhi}}  oxed{	ext{haryanvi}}  oxed{	ext{chhattisgarhi}} which are related to hindi and share its:  oxed{	ext{devanagari script}}. (source: indicgenbench-slides) when translating into these languages, the model frequently:  oxed{	ext{mixes in hindi words}} and produces:  oxed{	ext{incorrectly inflected verb forms}}. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.11 what does language bleeding mean? suppose the requested output language is: l_t=	ext{awadhi}. but the model generates: 	ext{awadhi} + 	ext{hindi} instead of pure awadhi. so:  oxed{ p(	ext{target-language token}) } is being contaminated by a closely related language's patterns. this is a conceptual interpretation of the observed behavior. 5.35.12 why are related languages especially vulnerable? because they may share:  oxed{	ext{script}} and:  oxed{	ext{linguistic structure}} but still have different:  oxed{	ext{vocabulary}} and:  oxed{	ext{morphology}} and:  oxed{	ext{inflection}} rules. so the model may learn: “this input belongs to a hindi-like region of representation space.” but fail to maintain the precise target-language boundary. the source explicitly identifies the shared-devanagari/related-language setting and the resulting hindi mixing. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.13 important distinction: related language ≠ same l