eq 	ext{language correctness} }. this is particularly important when evaluating generation in closely related languages. 5.35.16 failure mode 3 — polysemy the third failure is:  oxed{	ext{polysemy / word-sense selection}} a single source word can have multiple meanings depending on context. the deck gives a concrete example involving:  oxed{	ext{“hearing”}} which can refer to a: 	ext{court hearing} or: 	ext{listening}. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.17 what went wrong? in the konkani example, the same english word appears in two different senses. the model chooses the:  oxed{	ext{“listening”}} sense both times. but the first occurrence refers to:  oxed{	ext{a court hearing}}. (source: indicgenbench-slides) so the model failed to use context to disambiguate the word. 5.35.18 why is this important for machine translation? machine translation is not:  oxed{ 	ext{word} ightarrow 	ext{fixed dictionary translation} } it should be:  oxed{ 	ext{word + context} ightarrow 	ext{appropriate target meaning} }. this is the essence of polysemy. the same source word: w can represent: m_1,m_2,\ldots,m_k. the model must estimate:  oxed{ p(m_i\mid w,	ext{context}) } rather than always choosing the same m_i . that equation is general nlp reasoning, not a source-provided formula. 5.35.19 numerical polysemy example suppose a word has two possible senses: p(m_1\mid context)=0.8 p(m_2\mid context)=0.2. correct interpretation:  oxed{m_1}. but if the model uses a context-insensitive prior: p(m_1)=0.4, p(m_2)=0.6, it may choose:  oxed{m_2} incorrectly. again, this is an illustrative mathematical explanation, not a reported model probability. 5.35.20 failure mode 4 — missing information the same qualitative-analysis slide identifies another issue:  oxed{ 	ext{information omission} } the deck gives an assamese example where:  oxed{	ext{“to shop”}} is simply absent from the generated translation. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.21 what does omission mean mathematically? suppose source