ndi} ightarrow 	ext{awadhi/haryanvi/chhattisgarhi} } can cause hindi to bleed into the target output. (source: indicgenbench-slides) so linguistic similarity has two sides:  oxed{ 	ext{similarity} ightarrow 	ext{better transfer} } but also:  oxed{ 	ext{similarity} ightarrow 	ext{greater risk of confusion} }. that is a very important research insight. 5.35.26 numerical conceptual model of this tradeoff suppose linguistic relatedness gives: +10 points of transfer benefit. but language confusion causes: -4 points of bleeding-related degradation. then net: 10-4 =  oxed{+6}. this is purely an illustrative decomposition. the source does not provide separate numeric contributions for language bleeding. the key idea is that relatedness can be both:  oxed{	ext{an advantage}} and:  oxed{	ext{a source of interference}}. 5.35.27 connect hallucination with summarization crosssum-in is:  oxed{ 	ext{long source} ightarrow 	ext{short target output} }. that compression creates a difficult requirement:  oxed{ 	ext{remove redundancy without inventing facts} }. the qualitative result shows that the model can fail specifically on the second requirement. so summarization quality is not just: 	ext{shorter text}. it is:  oxed{ 	ext{compression} + 	ext{faithfulness} }. 5.35.28 connect polysemy to cross-lingual translation in machine translation: 	ext{source word} ightarrow 	ext{target word} is insufficient. the actual process is closer to:  oxed{ 	ext{source word} + 	ext{sentence context} + 	ext{discourse context} ightarrow 	ext{target meaning} }. the konkani “hearing” example demonstrates a failure when context isn't used correctly to choose between senses. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.29 connect omission to information preservation for translation, a good system should preserve:  oxed{ 	ext{who} + 	ext{what} + 	ext{when} + 	ext{where} + 	ext{relevant action} }. if an important action such as:  oxed{	ext{“to shop”}} disappears, the output may sound fluent while still being incomplete. that's an especially important lesson:  oxed{ 	ext{fluency} 
eq 	ext{faithfulness}. } the assamese example in the deck demonstrates exactly this point. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.30 the qualitative-analysis matrix you can organize the failures like this: failure what model does what should happen hallucination adds unsupported fact preserve source facts language bleeding uses hindi in another target language stay in requested language polysemy picks wrong word sense use contextual sense omission drops source information preserve relevant content (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.31 exam-style scenario suppose the source says: “the court scheduled a hearing for monday.” model translation interprets “hearing” as “listening.” classification:  oxed{	ext{polysemy error}} not hallucination. suppose instead the model writes: “the court scheduled a hearing for monday and the judge ordered five arrests.” when the source says nothing about arrests. classification:  oxed{	ext{hallucination}}. suppose: “the boy went to the shop and bought food.” becomes: “the boy went to the shop.” classification:  oxed{	ext{omission}}. suppose the target is awadhi but half the generated words are hindi. classification:  oxed{	ext{language bleeding}}. these distinctions are directly grounded in the failure categories shown by the deck. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.32 why this matters for model improvement different errors require different interventions. hallucination potential direction:  oxed{ 	ext{better source grounding / factuality control} } language bleeding potential direction:  oxed{ 	ext{better target-language conditioning / language-specific training} } polysemy potential direction:  oxed{ 	ext{better contextual semantic modeling} } omission potential direction:  oxed{ 	ext{better coverage/content preservation} } these are general remediation ideas. the supplied deck identifies the failure modes but does not prescribe a complete solution for each, so these improvement directions should be treated as model-level inference rather than source claims. 5.35.33 final section: licensing the deck closes with a practical but important issue:  oxed{ 	ext{dataset licensing} }. because indicgenbench builds on existing datasets, it inherits their licensing constraints. (source: indicgenbench-slides) the reported licenses are:  oxed{ 	ext{xquad-in, flores-in} ightarrow 	ext{cc by-sa 4.0} }  oxed{ 	ext{crosssum-in} ightarrow 	ext{cc by-nc-sa 4.0} }  oxed{ 	ext{xorqa-in} ightarrow 	ext{mit} } (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.34 why does source lineage affect licensing? because:  oxed{ 	ext{new benchmark} } doesn't necessarily mean:  oxed{ 	ext{new legal status}. } if a benchmark extends: 	ext{dataset a}, its derived data may inherit constraints from dataset a. so:  oxed{ 	ext{technical lineage} ightarrow 	ext{licensing lineage} }. the deck explicitly calls this another consequence of the translation-based approach. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.35 why should engineers care? suppose you build: 	ext{new multilingual dataset} from: 	ext{existing benchmark}. even if: 	ext{your annotations are new}, you still need to check:  oxed{ 	ext{license of original data} }. a dataset being publicly available does not automatically mean unrestricted commercial or derivative use. the exact legal obligations depend on the license. the deck's point is specifically that benchmark lineage determines these constraints. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.36 complete indicgenbench story we can now connect the whole benchmark from beginning to end. step 1 why build it?  oxed{ 	ext{indic generation quality was insufficiently measured} } step 2 how?  oxed{ 29	ext{ languages} + 5	ext{ generation tasks} } step 3 how construct data?  oxed{ 	ext{existing benchmarks} + 	ext{professional translation} } step 4 how score?  oxed{ 	ext{chrf} + 	ext{token-f1} } step 5 what happens quantitatively?  oxed{ 	ext{high}> 	ext{medium}> 	ext{low} } and:  oxed{ 	ext{indic generation}<	ext{english generation} } step 6 what actually goes wrong?  oxed{ 	ext{hallucination} + 	ext{language bleeding} + 	ext{polysemy errors} + 	ext{omissions} } (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.37 the deepest takeaway the most important lesson from the qualitative analysis is:  oxed{ 	ext{a multilingual model can produce fluent-looking text and still fail in several fundamentally different ways.} } it can:  oxed{ 	ext{invent facts} } while sounding fluent. it can:  oxed{ 	ext{mix languages} } while remaining understandable. it can:  oxed{ 	ext{choose the wrong meaning} } while producing grammatical text. and it can:  oxed{ 	ext{omit important source information} } while generating a concise output. therefore:  oxed{ 	ext{good generation} 
eq 	ext{fluency alone} } a robust multilingual generator needs:  oxed{ 	ext{faithfulness} + 	ext{language fidelity} + 	ext{semantic correctness} + 	ext{information preservation} }. that is the most important conclusion from the final indicgenbench slides. (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.38 final exam bank q1 a model inserts facts absent from the source.  oxed{	ext{hallucination}} q2 a model translates into awadhi but repeatedly inserts hindi words.  oxed{	ext{language bleeding}} q3 “hearing” is translated as “listening” even when the context indicates a court proceeding.  oxed{	ext{polysemy / sense-selection error}} q4 the source says “to shop,” but the translation omits that action.  oxed{	ext{information omission}} q5 why inspect outputs manually?  oxed{ 	ext{aggregate metrics tell you that performance is poor; qualitative analysis tells you what kind of errors produced the poor performance.} } (source: indicgenbench-slides) 5.35.39 course-level conclusion of the indic benchmark section we have now completed the major indic benchmark story:  oxed{ 	ext{indicxtreme} } measures multilingual understanding/nlu .  oxed{ 	ext{indicgenbench} } measures multilingual generation . together:  oxed{ 	ext{understand} + 	ext{generate} } give a much broader picture of indic-language llm capability. and both reveal the same fundamental reality:  oxed{ 	ext{language-resource imbalance remains a major obstacle} } even when multilingual or indic-focused models become substantially stronger. (source: indicgenbench-slides)

